findings.claim:
Sonar runs an open-core, LOC-priced model:
- an open-source Community Build;
- free cloud scanning for private projects up to 50k LOC and for public OSI-licensed repositories;
- paid tiers priced by lines of code (Cloud Team from $34/month for 100k LOC);
- some languages (ABAP, COBOL and others) only in the Enterprise plan.
In April 2022 Sonar raised $412M at a $4.7B valuation, with $175M ARR, gross margin above 90% and more than 16,000 commercial customers.

Sonar has bought its way into AI code verification and review:
- Tidelift (supply-chain security), announced 2024-12-17;
- AutoCodeRover (LLM agent that fixes code), 2025-02-19;
- Gitar (AI-native code review), 2026-05-21.
Terms were undisclosed for all three. Sonar now brands itself a leader in 'AI code verification and governance'.

Inference: cheap metrics, new-code gates, agent MCP hooks and budget framing are not defensible, because incumbents ship them already (tool-2, tool-3, fact-6). The assets that have protected vendors are calibration data (SIG, fact-3), third-party certification (fact-4), outcome-validation research (emp-1) and distribution (mkt-2). The domain-specific rule-pack gap (tool-5) is open. For Geiger, the plausible defensible bets are:
- a pluggable rule-pack format with hard architectural gates (hexagonal layers, ECS);
- a score whose per-PR delta is stable and explainable, which benchmark-relative recalibration breaks;
- over time, data showing that the score predicts agent and maintainer outcomes.
This rests on the cited findings. Market sizing for the gap is unverified.

